# Trivy misconfiguration findings accepted with a reason.
#
# Same rule as .checkov.yaml: an unexplained ignore is how a compliance repo
# quietly stops being one. Every entry names why the finding does not apply and
# where the equivalent control actually lives.

# S3 bucket does not have logging enabled.
# The access-log bucket IS the logging target. Pointing its own access logging
# at itself creates an unbounded recursion of log deliveries, and the replica
# would need a second access-log bucket in the DR region for records nobody
# reads. Auditor reads of the primary archive are logged (control LOG-007).
AVD-AWS-0089

# S3 bucket does not have versioning enabled.
# Reported against buckets whose versioning is a separate
# aws_s3_bucket_versioning resource, which Trivy does not always associate with
# the bucket. See controls LOG-005 and the aws_s3_bucket_versioning resources in
# terraform/modules/logging and terraform/modules/evidence.
AVD-AWS-0090

# IAM policy document uses a wildcard action.
# Triggered by the VPC endpoint policy in terraform/modules/network/endpoints.tf.
# An endpoint policy is intersected with the caller's IAM policy and the resource
# policy -- it can only narrow access, never grant it -- and an endpoint policy
# without a broad Allow blocks every call through the endpoint. The perimeter is
# the aws:ResourceOrgID Deny in the same document (control NET-007).
AVD-AWS-0057

# KMS key policy allows wildcard resource.
# A KMS key policy is only ever evaluated for the key it is attached to, so
# Resource "*" is the documented AWS idiom for "this key". Naming the key's own
# ARN is circular. Scope comes from the principal and aws:SourceArn /
# aws:SourceAccount / aws:PrincipalOrgID conditions (control ENC-004).
AVD-AWS-0132
